WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.198
Each request gets its own virtual thread.

2
00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:10.927
Ten thousand requests means ten thousand threads, and most of them are waiting on the network.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.130
Yet only a few OS threads run all of them.

4
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:14.996
Here is how.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.730
The JDK runs virtual threads on a ForkJoinPool of its own.

6
00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:23.691
Its workers are platform threads, called carrier threads.

7
00:00:23.691 --> 00:00:25.984
By default there is one per core.

8
00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:27.652
Say the machine has two.

9
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.887
A carrier takes a ready virtual thread from the queue and runs it.

10
00:00:31.887 --> 00:00:33.373
This is called mounting.

11
00:00:33.373 --> 00:00:37.399
The thread's top stack frames now sit on the carrier's own stack.

12
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.975
Now VT 1 calls a socket read.

13
00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:42.107
No data has arrived yet.

14
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.792
So the JDK unmounts it.

15
00:00:43.792 --> 00:00:49.567
It copies the frames into stack chunk objects on the heap, and the carrier is free again.

16
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.089
The carrier does not wait for the network.

17
00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:54.364
It mounts the next ready virtual thread.

18
00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:00.544
VT 2 calls sleep, so it unmounts the same way, and carrier 2 picks up VT 4.

19
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.533
Meanwhile, the JDK has registered the socket of VT 1 with a poller.

20
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:11.692
When data arrives, the poller unparks VT 1, and it goes back into the ready queue.

21
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.089
It waits its turn.

22
00:01:13.089 --> 00:01:18.632
VT 4 then blocks on a lock and unmounts, and carrier 2 mounts VT 5.

23
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.824
Then VT 3 finishes, and carrier 1 mounts VT 6.

24
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.742
VT 5 sends its reply and finishes.

25
00:01:25.742 --> 00:01:29.252
Now carrier 2 is free, and it mounts VT 1.

26
00:01:29.252 --> 00:01:35.187
The JDK copies its top frames back, and VT 1 carries on after the read.

27
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.334
VT 1 started on carrier 1 and now runs on carrier 2.

28
00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:40.498
That is normal.

29
00:01:40.498 --> 00:01:44.067
A virtual thread can come back on any carrier.

30
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.064
Your code never sees the switch.

31
00:01:47.064 --> 00:01:52.352
Thread.currentThread() returns the same virtual thread, before and after the read.

32
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.356
The one place the carrier shows is toString.

33
00:01:55.356 --> 00:01:57.694
The part after the at sign names it.

34
00:01:57.694 --> 00:01:59.511
Use that for debugging only.

35
00:01:59.511 --> 00:02:01.913
The format is not specified anywhere.

36
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.822
A parked virtual thread costs a little heap, not an OS thread.

37
00:02:05.822 --> 00:02:09.004
That is how two carriers can serve thousands of them.
